      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        "The outcome of a metaphyseal ulnar shortening osteotomy, reported by Sennwald as an alternative option for ulnar abutment syndrome, was evaluated with -regard to the functional results, pain relief and incidence of complications.",
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHOD",
          "content": "We conducted a review of 12 patients\u2008; measured parameters were DASH score, PRWE, VAS for pain, grip force and wrist motion."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Only disability improved significantly with a DASH score of 42 (p\u2008=\u20080.03). The function and pain assessment scores did not improve in 3 patients. We found no correlation between change in functional scores and other measured variables. Six patients were reoperated for prominent screw heads or new-onset distal radioulnar joint osteoarthritis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "All osteomies healed fast and uneventfully and disability improved in most patients but 25% did not improve and 50% were re-operated. Further investigation is needed to evaluate the value of the metaphyseal osteotomy as an alternative treatment for ulnar abutment syndrome."
        }
      ]},
